is \\(2n\\), which is divisible by \\(2n\\).\n\nAfter verifying for multiple cases and considering the constraints, it is concluded that there is exactly one unique arrangement up to rotation and reflection for any \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Let's consider the problem of arranging all natural numbers from 1 to \\(2n\\) in a circle such that each number is a divisor of the sum of its two adjacent numbers.\n2. First, we note that if the numbers do not alternate in parity (i.e., odd and even), then there will be at least two even numbers next to each other.\n3. Suppose two even numbers, \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), are adjacent. The sum of these numbers is also even (\\(a + b\\)), and \\(a\\) must be a divisor of \\(a + b\\). Since \\(a\\) is even, \\(a + b\\) must also be even, and \\(a\\) being even means that \\(b\\) must also be even. Continuing this chain, we realize that all numbers must be even, which is a contradiction since we have odd numbers in our set.\n4. Therefore, the numbers must alternate in parity around the circle.\n\n5. Consider the number \\(2n-1\\). Its neighbors must both be even, let's denote them as \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   a + b \\equiv 0 \\,(\\bmod \\, 2n-1)\n   \\]\n6. The only pairs of neighboring even numbers that meet this condition are:\n   \\[\n   a = 2n \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 2n-2,\n   \\]\n   since the maximum sum of two remaining numbers must equal \\(4n-2\\). This sum being divisible by \\(2n-1\\) means:\n   \\[\n   4n-2 = k(2n-1) \\quad \\text{for some integer} \\,k.\n   \\]\n   Because \\( 4n-2 =  2(2n-1) \\), we have \\(a = 2n\\) and \\(b = 2n-2\\).\n\n7. Without loss of generality, assume the numbers \\(2n, 2n-1, 2n-2\\) are placed in that specific descending order clockwise.\n\n8. Now we consider what would happen if we continued this pattern. The circle, if filled continually in descending order clockwise, will next place \\(2n-3\\) next to \\(2n-4\\), repeating roughly the same reasoning as above for all \\(k\\), where \\(k>1\\).\n\n9. Hence, the process will continue, and at the end of the reasoning, we see that this specific order (either clockwise or counterclockwise) is the unique solution. No other order respects the stipulated conditions regarding divisors.\n\nConclusion: \nThere is only 1 way to arrange the \\(2n\\) numbers in a circle such that every number is a divisor of the sum of its two neighbors, considering the rotations and reflections are treated equivalently.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1 \\text{ variant}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Show that $\\varphi\\left(p_{1}^{\\lambda_{1}} p_{2}^{\\lambda_{2}} \\ldots p_{n}^{\\lambda_{n}}\\right) = p_{1}^{\\lambda_{1}-1} p_{2}^{\\lambda_{2}-1} \\ldots p_{n}^{\\lambda_{n}-1} \\varphi\\left(p_{1}\\right) \\varphi\\left(p_{2}\\right) \\ldots \\varphi\\left(p_{n}\\right)$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to show that Euler's totient function \u03c6 applied to a number that's the product of prime powers, like p\u2081^\u03bb\u2081 p\u2082^\u03bb\u2082...p\u2099^\u03bb\u2099, is equal to p\u2081^(\u03bb\u2081-1) p\u2082^(\u03bb\u2082-1)...p\u2099^(\u03bb\u2099-1) multiplied by \u03c6(p\u2081)\u03c6(p\u2082)...\u03c6(p\u2099). Hmm, I remember that Euler's totient function is multiplicative for coprime integers, right? So if the primes are distinct, then the totient of the product is the product of the totients. But here, each prime is raised to a power. Let me recall the formula for \u03c6 when dealing with prime powers. \n\nFor a single prime power, \u03c6(p^k) = p^k - p^(k-1) = p^(k-1)(p - 1). So that's p raised to k-1 times (p - 1). Now, if we have multiple distinct primes each raised to a power, since they're coprime, \u03c6 should be multiplicative over them. So \u03c6(p\u2081^\u03bb\u2081 p\u2082^\u03bb\u2082...p\u2099^\u03bb\u2099) should be the product of \u03c6(p\u2081^\u03bb\u2081)\u03c6(p\u2082^\u03bb\u2082)...\u03c6(p\u2099^\u03bb\u2099). \n\nLet me write that out. \u03c6(p\u2081^\u03bb\u2081 p\u2082^\u03bb\u2082...p\u2099^\u03bb\u2099) = \u03c6(p\u2081^\u03bb\u2081)\u03c6(p\u2082^\u03bb\u2082)...\u03c6(p\u2099^\u03bb\u2099). Then, substituting the formula for each \u03c6(p_i^\u03bb_i), we get [p\u2081^(\u03bb\u2081 - 1)(p\u2081 - 1)][p\u2082^(\u03bb\u2082 - 1)(p\u2082 - 1)]...[p\u2099^(\u03bb\u2099 - 1)(p\u2099 - 1)]. \n\nNow, if I factor out each p_i^(\u03bb_i - 1) term, that would give me p\u2081^(\u03bb\u2081 - 1) p\u2082^(\u03bb\u2082 - 1)...p\u2099^(\u03bb\u2099 - 1) multiplied by (p\u2081 - 1)(p\u2082 - 1)...(p\u2099 - 1). But \u03c6(p_i) for a prime p_i is just p_i - 1, since \u03c6(p) = p - 1 when p is prime. So \u03c6(p\u2081)\u03c6(p\u2082)...\u03c6(p\u2099) = (p\u2081 - 1)(p\u2082 - 1)...(p\u2099 - 1). \n\nPutting that together, the original expression \u03c6(p\u2081^\u03bb\u2081 p\u2082^\u03bb\u2082...p\u2099^\u03bb\u2099) is indeed equal to p\u2081^(\u03bb\u2081 - 1) p\u2082^(\u03bb\u2082 - 1)...p\u2099^(\u03bb\u2099 - 1) multiplied by \u03c6(p\u2081)\u03c6(p\u2082)...\u03c6(p\u2099). That seems to check out. Let me verify with an example to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's take n = 2, p\u2081 = 2, \u03bb\u2081 = 3, p\u2082 = 3, \u03bb\u2082 = 2. \n\nCompute \u03c6(2\u00b3 * 3\u00b2). \u03c6(8*9) = \u03c6(72). The totient of 72: 72*(1 - 1/2)*(1 - 1/3) = 72*(1/2)*(2/3) = 72*(1/3) = 24. Alternatively, using the formula, \u03c6(2\u00b3) = 2\u00b3 - 2\u00b2 = 8 - 4 = 4, \u03c6(3\u00b2) = 3\u00b2 - 3\u00b9 = 9 - 3 = 6, so \u03c6(72) = 4*6 = 24. \n\nNow using the right-hand side: 2^(3-1) * 3^(2-1) * \u03c6(2)\u03c6(3) = 2\u00b2 * 3\u00b9 * (1)*(2) = 4*3*1*2 = 24. Yep, same result. So that works. Another example: take n = 1, just p^\u03bb. Then \u03c6(p^\u03bb) = p^(\u03bb - 1)(p - 1). The right-hand side would be p^(\u03bb - 1)\u03c6(p) = p^(\u03bb - 1)(p - 1), which matches. So the formula holds for single primes as well. \n\nTherefore, the general case works because the totient function is multiplicative over coprime integers, and each prime power's totient is p^(\u03bb - 1)(p - 1), which splits into p^(\u03bb - 1) times \u03c6(p). Multiplying all these together gives the product of p_i^(\u03bb_i - 1) terms multiplied by the product of \u03c6(p_i) terms. That's exactly the formula we're supposed to show. So breaking it down into the multiplicative property and the formula for prime powers seems to be the way to go. \n\nWait, but let me think again. Is there a case where the multiplicative property might not hold? Like if the exponents are more complicated? No, because even if the exponents are different, as long as the primes are distinct, the multiplicative property still applies. Since in the original expression, the primes p\u2081, p\u2082, ..., p\u2099 are distinct, their powers are coprime, so \u03c6 of the product is the product of \u03c6s. Therefore, combining all the \u03c6(p_i^\u03bb_i) terms as p_i^(\u03bb_i -1)(p_i -1) and then factoring out the p_i^(\u03bb_i -1) gives the desired result. \n\nSo, to recap, since \u03c6 is multiplicative over coprime numbers, and each prime power is coprime to the others, \u03c6 of the entire product is the product of \u03c6 of each prime power. Each \u03c6(p_i^\u03bb_i) is p_i^(\u03bb_i -1) multiplied by (p_i - 1), which is the same as \u03c6(p_i). Hence, when you take the product over all primes, you get the product of p_i^(\u03bb_i -1) terms multiplied by the product of \u03c6(p_i) terms. That's exactly the right-hand side of the equation given. Therefore, the identity holds. \n\nI think that covers it. Let me check another example to be thorough. Take n = 3, primes 2, 3, 5 with exponents 1, 2, 1. So the number is 2^1 * 3^2 *5^1 = 2*9*5 = 90. \u03c6(90) = \u03c6(2)\u03c6(9)\u03c6(5) = 1*6*4 = 24. Now, using the right-hand side formula: 2^(1-1)*3^(2-1)*5^(1-1) * \u03c6(2)\u03c6(3)\u03c6(5) = 2^0*3^1*5^0 * (1*2*4) = 1*3*1 * 8 = 24. Correct again. So the formula works here as well. \n\nTherefore, the proof seems solid. The key points are the multiplicative property of \u03c6 and the formula for \u03c6(p^k) for prime powers. Combining these gives the required identity.", "deepseek_solution": "To demonstrate the given identity for Euler's totient function \\(\\varphi\\), we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1: Use the Multiplicative Property of \\(\\varphi\\)**  \nEuler's totient function is multiplicative over coprime integers. For distinct primes \\(p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_n\\) and exponents \\(\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, \\ldots, \\lambda_n\\), the number \\(N = p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n}\\) has coprime prime-power factors. Thus:\n\\[\n\\varphi(N) = \\varphi(p_1^{\\lambda_1}) \\varphi(p_2^{\\lambda_2}) \\ldots \\varphi(p_n^{\\lambda_n}).\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Apply the Totient Formula for Prime Powers**  \nFor a single prime power \\(p_i^{\\lambda_i}\\), the totient is:\n\\[\n\\varphi(p_i^{\\lambda_i}) = p_i^{\\lambda_i} - p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1} = p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1}(p_i - 1).\n\\]\nSubstituting this into the product:\n\\[\n\\varphi(N) = \\prod_{i=1}^n p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1}(p_i - 1).\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Factorize the Product**  \nSeparate the terms \\(p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1}\\) and \\((p_i - 1)\\):\n\\[\n\\varphi(N) = \\left(\\prod_{i=1}^n p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1}\\right) \\left(\\prod_{i=1}^n (p_i - 1)\\right).\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Recognize \\(\\varphi(p_i)\\) for Primes**  \nFor a prime \\(p_i\\), \\(\\varphi(p_i) = p_i - 1\\). Thus:\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^n (p_i - 1) = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\varphi(p_i).\n\\]\n\n**Final Result**  \nCombining the factors:\n\\[\n\\varphi\\left(p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n}\\right) = \\left(\\prod_{i=1}^n p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1}\\right) \\left(\\prod_{i=1}^n \\varphi(p_i)\\right).\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe identity holds due to the multiplicative property of \\(\\varphi\\) and its explicit form for prime powers. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\varphi\\left(p_{1}^{\\lambda_{1}} p_{2}^{\\lambda_{2}} \\ldots p_{n}^{\\lambda_{n}}\\right) = p_{1}^{\\lambda_{1}-1} p_{2}^{\\lambda_{2}-1} \\ldots p_{n}^{\\lambda_{n}-1} \\varphi\\left(p_{1}\\right) \\varphi\\left(p_{2}\\right) \\ldots \\varphi\\left(p_{n}\\right)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the Euler\u2019s Totient Function:**\n   The Euler\u2019s totient function, denoted as \\(\\varphi(n)\\), is defined for a positive integer \\(n\\) and it counts the number of integers up to \\(n\\) that are coprime with \\(n\\). For a positive integer \\(n\\) with prime factorization \\(n = p_1^{a_1} p_2^{a_2} \\ldots p_k^{a_k}\\), the Euler\u2019s totient function is computed using the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\varphi(n) = n \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_1}\\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_2}\\right) \\ldots \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_k}\\right).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Given Expression:**\n   We need to show that:\n   \\[\n   \\varphi\\left( p_1^{\\lambda_{1}} p_2^{\\lambda_{2}} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_{n}} \\right) = p_1^{\\lambda_{1} - 1} p_2^{\\lambda_{2} - 1} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_{n} - 1} \\varphi(p_1) \\varphi(p_2) \\ldots \\varphi(p_n).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Applying the Totient Function:**\n   Starting with the given function, we apply the definition of the Euler\u2019s totient function:\n   \\[\n   \\varphi\\left( p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n} \\right) = \\left( p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n} \\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_1}\\right)\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_2}\\right) \\ldots \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_n}\\right).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplifying the Expression:**\n   We can rewrite the expression by distributing the product terms:\n   \\[\n   \\left( p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n} \\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_1}\\right)\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_2}\\right) \\ldots \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_n}\\right) = p_1^{\\lambda_1} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_1}\\right) p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_2}\\right) \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_n}\\right).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Breaking it Down Further:**\n   Each term \\(p_i^{\\lambda_i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_i}\\right)\\) can be simplified separately. Let\u2019s consider the generic term for \\(i\\)-th prime factor:\n   \\[\n   p_i^{\\lambda_i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{p_i}\\right) = p_i^{\\lambda_i} \\left(\\frac{p_i - 1}{p_i}\\right) = p_i^{\\lambda_i} \\cdot \\frac{p_i - 1}{p_i} = p_i^{\\lambda_i - 1} (p_i - 1).\n   \\]\n\n6. **Combining All Terms:**\n   By applying the above simplification for all prime factors, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\varphi\\left( p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n} \\right) = p_1^{\\lambda_1 - 1} (p_1 - 1) p_2^{\\lambda_2 - 1} (p_2 - 1) \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n - 1} (p_n - 1).\n   \\]\n\n7. **Relating Back to \\(\\varphi\\):**\n   We know that \\(\\varphi(p) = p - 1\\) for any prime \\(p\\). Therefore the expression simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\varphi(p_1^{\\lambda_1} p_2^{\\lambda_2} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n}) = p_1^{\\lambda_1 - 1} \\varphi(p_1) p_2^{\\lambda_2 - 1} \\varphi(p_2) \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_n - 1} \\varphi(p_n).\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\varphi\\left( p_1^{\\lambda_{1}} p_2^{\\lambda_{2}} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_{n}} \\right) = p_1^{\\lambda_{1} - 1} p_2^{\\lambda_{2} - 1} \\ldots p_n^{\\lambda_{n} - 1} \\varphi(p_1) \\varphi(p_2) \\ldots \\varphi(p_n)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find \\(\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left(1+\\operatorname{tg}^{2} x\\right)^{2 \\operatorname{ctg}^{2} x}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the limit as x approaches 0 of (1 + tan\u00b2x) raised to the power of 2 cot\u00b2x. Hmm, let's start by recalling some trigonometric identities and limit properties that might help here. \n\nFirst, I remember that tan x is sin x / cos x, and cot x is cos x / sin x. So tan\u00b2x would be sin\u00b2x / cos\u00b2x, and cot\u00b2x is cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x. Maybe I can express everything in terms of sine and cosine to simplify? Let me write that down:\n\ntan\u00b2x = sin\u00b2x / cos\u00b2x\ncot\u00b2x = cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x\n\nSo the expression becomes (1 + sin\u00b2x / cos\u00b2x) raised to the power of 2*(cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x). Let's simplify the base first. 1 + tan\u00b2x is actually a known identity. Wait, isn't 1 + tan\u00b2x equal to sec\u00b2x? Yes! So 1 + tan\u00b2x = sec\u00b2x. Therefore, the base of the exponent is sec\u00b2x. So now the expression is (sec\u00b2x)^(2 cot\u00b2x).\n\nBut sec\u00b2x is 1 / cos\u00b2x, so substituting that in, we have (1 / cos\u00b2x)^(2 cot\u00b2x). Let's see. But cot\u00b2x is cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x, so substituting that into the exponent gives:\n\n(1 / cos\u00b2x)^(2*(cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x)) = [ (1 / cos\u00b2x) ]^(2 cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x )\n\nHmm, maybe we can rewrite this as e raised to the natural logarithm of the expression. Since the limit is of the form [f(x)]^{g(x)}, we can use the identity that lim [f(x)]^{g(x)} = e^{lim g(x) * (f(x) - 1)} when f(x) approaches 1 and g(x) approaches infinity. But I need to check if that applies here. Alternatively, using the standard limit formula: lim_{x\u21920} (1 + kx)^{(1/x)} = e^k. But here, the exponent is 2 cot\u00b2x, which as x approaches 0, cot\u00b2x approaches infinity because cot x is cos x / sin x, and as x approaches 0, sin x approaches 0, so cot x approaches infinity. Therefore, the exponent is going to infinity, and the base is (1 + tan\u00b2x). Let me compute the base as x approaches 0.\n\nWhen x approaches 0, tan x approaches x, so tan\u00b2x approaches x\u00b2. Therefore, 1 + tan\u00b2x approaches 1 + x\u00b2. So the base is approaching 1, and the exponent is approaching infinity. So this is a 1^infinity indeterminate form. For such cases, the standard technique is to take the natural logarithm, find the limit of the logarithm, and then exponentiate the result.\n\nLet me set L = lim_{x\u21920} (1 + tan\u00b2x)^{2 cot\u00b2x}. Then ln L = lim_{x\u21920} 2 cot\u00b2x * ln(1 + tan\u00b2x). So if I can compute this limit, then L = e^{result}.\n\nSo let's compute ln L = lim_{x\u21920} 2 cot\u00b2x * ln(1 + tan\u00b2x).\n\nFirst, note that cot\u00b2x = (cos\u00b2x)/(sin\u00b2x). Let's substitute tan\u00b2x = sin\u00b2x / cos\u00b2x. So tan\u00b2x = (sin x / cos x)^2. Let me denote t = tan x. Then as x approaches 0, t approaches 0. So maybe substituting t = tan x would make this easier. Let's try that substitution.\n\nLet t = tan x. Then as x approaches 0, t approaches 0. Also, cot x = 1 / tan x = 1/t. Therefore, cot\u00b2x = 1/t\u00b2. Also, tan\u00b2x = t\u00b2. So ln(1 + tan\u00b2x) becomes ln(1 + t\u00b2). Therefore, the expression for ln L becomes:\n\nlim_{t\u21920} 2 * (1/t\u00b2) * ln(1 + t\u00b2)\n\nSo this simplifies to 2 * lim_{t\u21920} [ln(1 + t\u00b2) / t\u00b2]\n\nNow, I recall that as u approaches 0, ln(1 + u) is approximately u - u\u00b2/2 + u\u00b3/3 - ... So ln(1 + u) \u2248 u when u is near 0. Therefore, if u = t\u00b2, then ln(1 + t\u00b2) \u2248 t\u00b2 - t^4/2 + ... So dividing by t\u00b2 gives approximately 1 - t\u00b2/2 + ... Therefore, as t approaches 0, ln(1 + t\u00b2)/t\u00b2 approaches 1. Therefore, the limit becomes 2 * 1 = 2. Therefore, ln L = 2, so L = e\u00b2.\n\nWait, so the answer is e squared? Let me check this again step by step to make sure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\nStarting from the original limit:\n\nlim_{x\u21920} (1 + tan\u00b2x)^{2 cot\u00b2x}\n\nWe recognize the 1^infinity form. Take the natural logarithm:\n\nln L = lim_{x\u21920} 2 cot\u00b2x * ln(1 + tan\u00b2x)\n\nExpress cot\u00b2x as 1/tan\u00b2x:\n\n= lim_{x\u21920} 2 * (1/tan\u00b2x) * ln(1 + tan\u00b2x)\n\nLet t = tan x, so as x\u21920, t\u21920. Then tan\u00b2x = t\u00b2, and 1/tan\u00b2x = 1/t\u00b2. So substitute:\n\n= lim_{t\u21920} 2 * (1/t\u00b2) * ln(1 + t\u00b2)\n\nNow, the limit becomes 2 * lim_{t\u21920} ln(1 + t\u00b2)/t\u00b2\n\nAs t\u21920, ln(1 + t\u00b2) ~ t\u00b2 - t^4/2 + ..., so ln(1 + t\u00b2)/t\u00b2 ~ 1 - t\u00b2/2 + ..., which approaches 1. Therefore, the limit is 2*1 = 2. Therefore, ln L = 2, so L = e\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, the limit is e squared.\n\nAlternatively, to verify this, perhaps using another method. Let's see. Another approach is to express the original expression in terms of exponentials and logarithms and then apply Taylor series expansions.\n\nAlternatively, recall that lim_{x\u21920} (1 + a x^2)^{b / x^2} = e^{a b}, assuming a and b are constants. Let me check if this can be applied here.\n\nLet's see. In our case, the base is (1 + tan\u00b2x). As x\u21920, tan x ~ x, so tan\u00b2x ~ x\u00b2. Therefore, the base is 1 + x\u00b2 + higher order terms. The exponent is 2 cot\u00b2x. Since cot x ~ 1/x as x\u21920, so cot\u00b2x ~ 1/x\u00b2, so the exponent is 2 / x\u00b2. Therefore, the expression is similar to (1 + x\u00b2)^{2 / x\u00b2}, which as x\u21920 would approach e^{x\u00b2 * 2 / x\u00b2} = e^2. Therefore, this aligns with our previous conclusion.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the method, we get the limit as e squared. Therefore, the answer should be e\u00b2, which is e squared.\n\nLet me check once again for any possible mistakes. Did I mix up any identities? Let's see:\n\n1 + tan\u00b2x = sec\u00b2x, correct. So (sec\u00b2x)^{2 cot\u00b2x}. Then sec\u00b2x is 1 / cos\u00b2x, so (1 / cos\u00b2x)^{2 cot\u00b2x} = [ (1 / cos\u00b2x) ]^{2 (cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x)}. Let's compute the exponent: 2 (cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x). So [1 / cos\u00b2x]^{2 cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x} = [ (1 / cos\u00b2x)^{cos\u00b2x} ]^{2 / sin\u00b2x} = [ (1 / cos^{2 cos\u00b2x} x ) ]^{2 / sin\u00b2x}. Hmm, this seems more complicated, but maybe we can take logs here as well.\n\nAlternatively, let's note that as x\u21920, cos x ~ 1 - x\u00b2/2, so cos\u00b2x ~ 1 - x\u00b2, but maybe that's not necessary. Alternatively, since as x approaches 0, cos x approaches 1, so 1 / cos\u00b2x approaches 1. But the exponent is 2 cot\u00b2x, which is 2 (cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x). Since sin x ~ x, cos\u00b2x ~ 1 - x\u00b2. So cos\u00b2x / sin\u00b2x ~ (1 - x\u00b2)/x\u00b2 ~ 1/x\u00b2 - 1. Therefore, 2 cot\u00b2x ~ 2/x\u00b2 - 2. But as x approaches 0, 2/x\u00b2 dominates, so exponent ~ 2/x\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, we have (1 + tan\u00b2x)^{2 cot\u00b2x} ~ (1 + x\u00b2)^{2 / x\u00b2} ~ e^{2}, since lim_{x\u21920} (1 + x\u00b2)^{1/x\u00b2} = e. Therefore, raising this to the power of 2 gives e\u00b2. So this also confirms the result.\n\nTherefore, all methods converge to the same answer, e\u00b2. So I think this is correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe limit is \\boxed{e^2}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the limit \\(\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left(1+\\operatorname{tg}^{2} x\\right)^{2 \\operatorname{ctg}^{2} x}\\), we start by recognizing the trigonometric identities and the indeterminate form \\(1^\\infty\\).\n\n1. **Trigonometric Identities**:\n   - \\(\\tan^2 x = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}\\)\n   - \\(\\cot^2 x = \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{\\sin^2 x}\\)\n   - \\(1 + \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\\)\n\n2. **Rewrite the Expression**:\n   - The expression becomes \\((\\sec^2 x)^{2 \\cot^2 x}\\).\n   - \\(\\sec^2 x = \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x}\\), so the expression is \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x}\\right)^{2 \\cot^2 x}\\).\n\n3. **Substitute \\(t = \\tan x\\)**:\n   - As \\(x \\rightarrow 0\\), \\(t \\rightarrow 0\\).\n   - \\(\\cot^2 x = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 x} = \\frac{1}{t^2}\\).\n   - The expression becomes \\(\\left(1 + t^2\\right)^{2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{t^2}}\\).\n\n4. **Natural Logarithm Transformation**:\n   - Let \\(L = \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\left(1 + \\tan^2 x\\right)^{2 \\cot^2 x}\\).\n   - Taking the natural logarithm: \\(\\ln L = \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} 2 \\cot^2 x \\cdot \\ln(1 + \\tan^2 x)\\).\n\n5. **Simplify the Limit**:\n   - Substitute \\(t = \\tan x\\): \\(\\ln L = \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 0} 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{t^2} \\cdot \\ln(1 + t^2)\\).\n   - Use the approximation \\(\\ln(1 + t^2) \\approx t^2\\) as \\(t \\rightarrow 0\\): \\(\\frac{\\ln(1 + t^2)}{t^2} \\approx 1\\).\n\n6. **Evaluate the Limit**:\n   - \\(\\ln L = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(L = e^2\\).\n\nThus, the limit is \\(\\boxed{e^2}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "To solve the limit \\(\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left(1+\\operatorname{tg}^{2} x\\right)^{2 \\operatorname{ctg}^{2} x}\\), let's follow these steps:\n\n1. We start by recognizing the given expressions \\(\\operatorname{tg} x = \\tan x\\) and \\(\\operatorname{ctg} x = \\cot x\\). By definition, \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\).\n\n2. Rewrite the original limit using the identity \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left(1+\\tan^2 x\\right)^{2 (\\cot^2 x)} = \\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left(1+\\tan^2 x\\right)^{2 \\left(\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 x}\\right)}\n\\]\n\n3. Simplify the exponent:\n\n\\[\n= \\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left(1+\\tan^2 x\\right)^{\\frac{2}{\\tan^2 x}}\n\\]\n\n4. Further simplifying, the expression inside the limit can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n= \\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} \\left[ \\left(1 + \\tan^2 x \\right)^{\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 x}} \\right]^2\n\\]\n\n5. By recognizing the limit form \\(\\lim_{y \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{y}\\right)^y = e\\), we observe that as \\(x \\rightarrow 0\\), \\(\\tan^2 x \\rightarrow 0\\). Thus, we use the standard limit property:\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\left(1 + \\tan^2 x\\right)^{\\frac{1}{\\tan^2 x}} = e\n\\]\n\n6. As this is a standard exponential limit, we substitute this result back into our expression:\n\n\\[\n= \\left[ e \\right]^2 = e^2\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nTherefore, the limit is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{e^2}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "I saw the following trick by a magician. A spectator randomly chose 5 cards from a 52-card French deck and handed them to the magician's assistant. (The assistant was a regular partner of the magician, so they could have prearranged something earlier.) The assistant looked at the 5 cards and handed 4 of them, one by one, to the magician. Then the magician, without any further information, named the fifth card.\n\nAt the end of the trick, the magician tried to convince the mathematically-inclined spectators in the following way:\n\n\"My assistant showed me four cards, and these four cards can be chosen arbitrarily from the deck. Thus, they could only provide information to me through the order in which the cards were presented. As is well known, 4 cards can be ordered in $4! = 24$ ways, but the fifth card could be any of $52 - 4 = 48$. So, precisely one bit of information is missing, hence the need for a miracle.\"\n\nIs it true that supernatural abilities are indeed required for the trick?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to figure out whether the magician's trick requires supernatural abilities or if there's a mathematical or logical explanation behind it. The magician claims that since there are 48 possible fifth cards and only 24 possible orderings of the four cards, there's a missing bit of information, implying a miracle is needed. But maybe there's a clever encoding method they're using.\n\nFirst, let's break down the numbers. The magician receives four cards, and the fifth card has to be determined. The assistant can arrange the four cards in 4! = 24 different orders. The number of possible remaining cards is 52 - 4 = 48. The magician's argument is that 24 orderings can't encode 48 possibilities, so there's a discrepancy. But wait, 48 is exactly twice 24. So, actually, if you can somehow encode two different pieces of information with each ordering, then 24 orderings could cover 48 possibilities. Hmm, maybe there's a way to split the problem into two parts.\n\nAnother thought: the four cards themselves aren't just ordered; their selection might also carry information. The assistant chooses which four cards to show out of the five. So, perhaps the choice of which card to exclude also encodes some information. If there are five choices for which card to exclude, and each exclusion can be combined with 24 orderings, then the total number of possible messages would be 5 * 24 = 120. Since 120 is much larger than 48, that would be more than enough. Wait, but the magician said that the four cards could be chosen arbitrarily, so maybe the assistant can't choose which card to exclude. Wait, no\u2014the assistant is the one who hands over the four cards. So the assistant can choose which four to present, thereby selecting which one to exclude. Therefore, the key is that the assistant can choose which card to keep hidden, and then use the ordering of the four to encode additional information.\n\nLet me check the numbers again. If there are five possible cards to exclude, and for each exclusion, 24 possible orderings, then 5 * 24 = 120. But the number of possible fifth cards is 48. Since 120 is more than 48, the assistant can use the choice of the excluded card along with the ordering to encode which card is the fifth one. Therefore, no supernatural abilities are needed\u2014it's a mathematical trick using combinatorial encoding.\n\nWait, but how exactly does that work? Let's think step by step. Suppose you have five cards. The assistant picks one to hide and arranges the remaining four in a specific order. The magician knows the possible fifth card by decoding both the identity of the hidden card (based on which four are shown) and the order of the four cards. But how is the hidden card determined?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the four cards are used to encode a number between 1 and 24, and the fifth card is determined by some prearranged system. But even then, since there are 48 possibilities, how do you cover all of them?\n\nAnother approach: maybe the four cards not only provide an order but also use their ranks and suits in a way that allows the magician to calculate the fifth card. For instance, using modular arithmetic or some kind of parity. Since there are 52 cards in a deck, each card can be uniquely identified by its rank (13 possibilities) and suit (4 possibilities). \n\nWait, here's an idea. Let's suppose the five cards are all part of a mathematical structure that allows the assistant and magician to agree on an ordering. For example, in any set of five cards, there must be at least two cards of the same suit (by the pigeonhole principle, since there are four suits). The assistant could use one of those two cards to indicate the suit of the hidden card. Then, the other three cards can be ordered to encode a number that represents the rank. Since there are 13 ranks, you need to encode a number from 1 to 13. With three cards, you can have 3! = 6 permutations, which isn't enough. Wait, but maybe using the two cards of the same suit: the assistant can choose one to be the reference card, and the other to be the hidden card. Then, the difference in ranks between the reference and the hidden card can be encoded using the order of the remaining three cards. Since the maximum difference modulo 13 is 6 (since 13 is odd, the furthest apart two ranks can be is 6 steps in either direction), and 3! = 6 permutations, that could work. For example, if the reference card is the first one, the hidden card is of the same suit, and the difference in rank (using a circular order, Ace through King) is between 1 and 6. The order of the remaining three cards can encode that difference. Then, the magician knows to look for the suit of the first card, compute the difference based on the order of the next three, and add that difference to the reference card's rank modulo 13 to find the hidden card. That seems plausible.\n\nLet me check if this works. Suppose you have five cards. At least two are of the same suit. The assistant picks one of those two to be the hidden card and the other to be the reference. Then, the assistant arranges the four cards such that the reference card is first. The remaining three cards are ordered to represent a number from 1 to 6, which gives the offset from the reference card's rank. Since the maximum distance between two ranks in the same suit is 6 (since 13/2 = 6.5, so the shorter distance is at most 6), the offset can be uniquely determined. Then, the magician sees the first card's suit, knows the hidden card is of that suit, calculates the offset from the reference card using the permutation of the next three cards, and deduces the hidden card. \n\nBut wait, what if there's more than two cards of the same suit? Then the assistant has multiple choices for which two to use. Maybe they have a priority system, like choosing the suit with the smallest number of cards, or the highest-ranking cards, etc. Alternatively, they might use the first occurring suit in a predefined order (like alphabetical: clubs, diamonds, hearts, spades) that has at least two cards. Then, among those, select the reference and hidden card based on some order, like ranking.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the assistant and magician use a different encoding. For example, using the concept of permutations to encode a number. Since there are 24 possible orderings of four cards, and 48 possibilities for the fifth card, each ordering can correspond to two different cards. But how to map this? The key would be to partition the 48 remaining cards into 24 pairs, and the ordering communicates which one of the pair is the fifth card. However, this requires that for any five-card hand, the fifth card is always in one of two possible cards determined by the four shown. But how can that be guaranteed?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the assistant can use the four cards to encode a binary choice (the missing bit) plus a number from 1 to 24. Wait, but how?\n\nAnother angle: The total number of possible five-card hands is C(52,5) = 2,598,960. The assistant is effectively mapping each five-card hand to a four-card sequence with an ordering. The number of four-card sequences with ordering is P(52,4) = 52*51*50*49 = 6,497,400. Since this is larger than the number of five-card hands, it's possible to have an injective mapping. But in reality, the four cards are selected from the five, so the mapping is constrained. Specifically, for each five-card hand, the assistant can choose which four to present, and their order. The number of possible messages per five-card hand is 5 (choices of excluded card) * 4! (orderings) = 120. Since 120 is greater than 48 (the number of possible remaining cards), this allows for enough different messages to encode the fifth card. Therefore, as long as the assistant and magician have a prearranged system to map each possible fifth card (given the four shown) to one of the 120 possible messages (5 positions * 24 orderings), the trick can be done without any supernatural ability.\n\nTo put it another way, the magician's argument that four cards can only provide 24 bits of information is flawed because it ignores the choice of which card to exclude. The combination of the excluded card and the ordering provides more than enough information to specify the fifth card.\n\nSo, in conclusion, the trick doesn't require supernatural abilities. It uses a mathematical encoding scheme where both the choice of which card to exclude and the order of the four presented cards are used to convey the necessary information. This allows covering all 48 possible remaining cards with the 120 possible messages (5*24), which is more than sufficient. Therefore, the magician's claim of needing a miracle is just part of the act, and the trick is based on clever mathematics.", "deepseek_solution": "The magician's assertion that supernatural abilities are required is a misdirection. The trick relies on a mathematical encoding scheme that cleverly utilizes both the selection and ordering of the four cards. Here's the breakdown:\n\n1. **Information Capacity**:  \n   - The assistant can choose **which card to exclude** (5 possibilities) and **order the remaining four** (4! = 24 permutations).  \n   - Total encoding capacity: \\(5 \\times 24 = 120\\) distinct messages.  \n   - This exceeds the 48 possible fifth cards, providing ample information to uniquely identify the hidden card.\n\n2. **Key Encoding Strategy**:  \n   - **Suit Agreement**: By the pigeonhole principle, at least two cards in the five share a suit. The assistant uses one of these to establish the hidden card\u2019s suit.  \n   - **Rank Offset**: The assistant selects a reference card (same suit) and encodes the hidden card\u2019s rank as an offset (1\u20136) via the permutation of the remaining three cards. Since 3! = 6 permutations, this covers all possible offsets.  \n\n3. **Algorithmic Steps**:  \n   - **Step 1**: Identify two cards of the same suit. Choose the reference card based on a prearranged order (e.g., higher rank).  \n   - **Step 2**: Compute the circular rank difference (modulo 13) between the reference and hidden card.  \n   - **Step 3**: Encode this difference (1\u20136) using the permutation of the remaining three cards.  \n   - **Step 4**: The magician decodes the permutation to recover the offset and deduces the hidden card\u2019s rank.  \n\n**Conclusion**:  \nThe trick is mathematically grounded, leveraging combinatorial encoding and modular arithmetic. No supernatural elements are involved\u2014just a prearranged system and careful calculation. The magician\u2019s \u201cmiracle\u201d claim is theatrical flair.  \n\n**Final Answer**  \n\\boxed{\\text{No, the trick can be achieved through a mathematical encoding scheme without supernatural abilities.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nThe solution does not require supernatural abilities to perform the trick. We will outline two distinct methods to determine the fifth card.\n\n#### Method 1:\nIn a standard deck of French cards, the cards come in four different suits. Hence, among the selected five cards, there must be at least two cards of the same suit. The assistant can use the first card to communicate the suit of the fifth card, agreeing beforehand with the magician that the fifth card will have the same suit as the first one shown.\n\n1. Number the cards within each suit using the standard values from 1 to 13: Ace = 1, Two = 2, ..., Ten = 10, Jack = 11, Queen = 12, King = 13.\n2. Let's denote the value of the last card to be shown as \\( x \\) and the value of the first card as \\( y \\). Since both the first and fifth cards have the same suit, \\( 1 \\leq x \\neq y \\leq 13 \\).\n\nNow the assistant needs to communicate the exact rank (value) of the fifth card \\( x \\) using the remaining three cards.\n\n3. Assign an order to the suits, for example, using the bridge ranking: Clubs (lowest), Diamonds, Hearts, Spades (highest).\n4. This way, any pair of cards can be uniquely ordered.\n5. Using the lexicographic ordering, there are \\( 3! = 6 \\) permutations of the three middle cards. Therefore, the assistant has 6 different permutations to convey information, specifying an integer \\( s \\) between 1 and 6.\n6. We need to communicate \\( x \\) values ranging from 1 to 13, excluding \\( y \\), so it's necessary to determine \\( 1 \\leq x \\leq 13 \\) through \\( y \\) and \\( s \\).\n\nBy arranging the cards and calculating the difference \\( b - a \\) (where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the values of two cards: \\( y \\) and \\( x \\), respectively), the assistant encodes the distance modulo 13:\n- If \\( b - a \\leq 6 \\), assign \\( y = a \\) and \\( s = b - a \\); thus \\( x = b \\).\n- If \\( b - a > 6 \\), assign \\( y = b \\) and \\( s = 13 - (b - a) \\); then \\( x = a \\).\n\nThis method ensures \\( 1 \\leq s \\leq 6 \\). The value of the fifth card \\( x \\) can be determined by \\( x \\equiv (y + s) \\mod 13 \\).\n\n#### Method 2:\nIf there is an identifiable reference point in the first four cards, using the cyclical numbering mod 13, the assistant can directly communicate the information.\n\n1. Rank the cards from 1 to 52, arranged: Clubs Ace = 1 to Spades Kin