ir sum is 1 (from the coefficient of x\u00b2), and their product is -1 (from the constant term). So here we have three irrational numbers whose sum is 1 and product is -1, both integers. So yes, such numbers exist.\n\nBut wait, the question is asking if there exist three irrational numbers with sum and product as integers. So in this case, the roots of that cubic would satisfy the condition. Therefore, the answer is yes. But maybe I can think of a more explicit example.\n\nAlternatively, let's think of specific numbers. For example, take cube roots. Suppose a=\u221b2, b=\u221b4, c= something. Wait, but their sum would be \u221b2 + \u221b4 + c. That seems hard to make into an integer. Alternatively, maybe use numbers like \u221a2, \u221a3, and then some other number. But their sum would be \u221a2 + \u221a3 + x = integer. Then x would need to be integer - \u221a2 - \u221a3, which is irrational. Then their product would be \u221a2 * \u221a3 * x = \u221a6 * x. If x is (integer - \u221a2 - \u221a3), then the product would be \u221a6*(integer - \u221a2 - \u221a3). For that to be an integer, \u221a6*(integer) - \u221a12 - \u221a18. But those terms are all irrational. So unless the terms cancel out, which seems unlikely. For example, if the integer part cancels the other terms. But \u221a12 is 2\u221a3 and \u221a18 is 3\u221a2. So unless those terms are subtracted by something, but in the product, they are being subtracted. So \u221a6*k - 2\u221a3 - 3\u221a2. That's a sum of three irrationals, which is unlikely to be an integer. So maybe this approach isn't the way to go.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use numbers that are designed to cancel out their irrational parts when multiplied. For example, take a = 1 + \u221a2, b = 1 - \u221a2, and then c = some number. Then a + b + c = (1 + \u221a2) + (1 - \u221a2) + c = 2 + c. If we set 2 + c = integer, then c has to be an integer minus 2, which would make c rational. But we need c to be irrational. So that doesn't work. Alternatively, 