e terms C\u2080z\u207f and C\u2099 are aligned in the complex plane, so their magnitudes add up. \n\nLet me think. If we can choose z such that C\u2080z\u207f and C\u2099 are in the same direction (i.e., have the same argument), then |C\u2080z\u207f + C\u2099| = |C\u2080z\u207f| + |C\u2099| = |C\u2080||z|\u207f + |C\u2099|. But since |z| \u2264 1, the maximum of this expression would be when |z| = 1, giving |C\u2080| + |C\u2099|. However, this only accounts for two terms: the leading term and the constant term. The polynomial has other terms as well, so how do they affect this?\n\nIf we set z such that all the terms add constructively, maybe we can get a larger sum. But that might be difficult because the other coefficients could have different arguments. Alternatively, perhaps using an averaging argument or integrating around the unit circle and applying the mean value theorem? Let me explore that.\n\nConsider integrating |f(z)| over the unit circle |z|=1. If we can show that the average value of |f(z)| is at least |C\u2080| + |C\u2099|, then there must exist a point where |f(z)| is at least that average. But I'm not sure if the average is easy to compute here. Alternatively, maybe using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality or some other inequality.\n\nWait, another thought: consider evaluating f(z) at specific points. Let's look at z = 1 and z = -1. Wait, but those are real numbers. Since coefficients are complex, maybe z on the unit circle such that z\u207f is aligned with C\u2080 and C\u2099. For example, suppose we take z such that z\u207f = \\overline{C\u2080}/|C\u2080|, which would make C\u2080z\u207f = |C\u2080|. Then, if we can also adjust z so that the constant term C\u2099 is aligned with this, but z is a complex number on the unit circle. However, z\u207f is determined once z is chosen, so maybe it's not possible to align both C\u2080z\u207f and C\u2099 unless they have the same argument.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the Maximum Modulus Principle in reverse.