sing:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a_k} - \\frac{1}{a_{k+1}} > \\frac{1}{n + 1}.\n   \\]\n3. **Stronger Lower Bound**: Summing gives:\n   \\[\n   2 - \\frac{1}{a_n} > \\frac{n}{n + 1} \\implies a_n > \\frac{n + 1}{n + 2}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\frac{n + 1}{n + 2} > 1 - \\frac{1}{n}\\) for all \\(n \\geq 1\\), the required bound follows.\n\n### Conclusion:\nCombining both results, we conclude:\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{1}{n} < a_n < 1.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{1 - \\frac{1}{n} < a_n < 1}", "ground_truth_solution": "To prove \\(1 - \\frac{1}{n} < a_{n} < 1\\), we start by establishing some properties of the sequence \\(\\{a_{k}\\}\\).\n\n1. **Monotonicity:**\n   We first show that the sequence is increasing: for \\( k = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1 \\),\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1} = a_{k} + \\frac{1}{n} a_{k}^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Given \\( a_{0} = \\frac{1}{2} \\) and each subsequent term \\( a_{k+1} \\) includes an additional positive term \\( \\frac{1}{n} a_{k}^{2} \\), it's clear that \\( a_{k+1} > a_{k} \\) for all \\( k \\), proving:\n   \\[\n   0 < a_{0} < a_{1} < a_{2} < \\cdots < a_{n}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper Bound:**\n   We now aim to show \\( a_{n} < 1 \\). Consider the recursive definition:\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1} = a_{k} \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{n} a_{k} \\right).\n   \\]\n   Since \\( a_{k} < 1 \\) implies \\( 1 + \\frac{1}{n} a_{k} < 1 + \\frac{1}{n} a_{k+1} \\), we calculate:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a_{k}} < \\frac{1}{a_{k+1}} + \\frac{1}{n}.\n   \\]\n   By induction:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a_n} > \\frac{1}{a_{n-1}} - \\frac{1}{n} > \\frac{1}{a_{n-2}} - \\frac{2}{n} > \\cdots > \\frac{1}{a_0} - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1.\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   a_{n} < 1.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lower Bound:**\n   We next show \\( a_{n} > 1 - \\frac{1}{n} \\). Starting from the recursive relationship again:\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1} = a_{k} + \\frac{1}{n} a_{k}^{2},\n   \\]\n   consider the expressions in terms of the variable \\( \\frac{1}{a_k} \\).\n   Using the identity \\( \\frac{a_{k+1}}{n+1} = \\frac{a_{k}}{n} \\left( 1 + \\frac{a_{k}}{n+1}