sqrt{3}}{2} \\), which implies \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\).\n\nWe verify this critical point by checking the second derivative:\n\\[\ny''(t) = \\frac{2(1 + 3t^2)}{(1 - t^2)^3} + \\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{t^3}\n\\]\nEvaluating the second derivative at \\( t = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\), we find it is positive, confirming a local minimum. Since the function tends to infinity as \\( t \\) approaches 0 or 1, this local minimum is the global minimum.\n\nSubstituting \\( t = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) back into the function:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{1}{1 - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2} + \\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} = 4 + 24 = 28\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of the function is \\(\\boxed{28}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven \\( x \\in \\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\), we are to find the minimum value of the function \n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 x} + \\frac{12 \\sqrt{3}}{\\cos x}. \\]\n\n1. **Rewriting the function in a more manipulable form:**\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 x} + 12\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos x}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Scaling and grouping the terms:**\n   \\[\n   y = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{16 \\sin^2 x} \\right) + \\frac{12 \\sqrt{3}}{\\cos x}.\n   \\]\n   \n   To apply the AM-GM inequality effectively, recognize how to group the terms appropriately.\n\n3. **Applying the AM-GM inequality:**\n   We know that for any two positive numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a + b}{2} \\geq \\sqrt{ab} \\implies a + b \\geq 2\\sqrt{ab}.\n   \\]\n\n   We'll group and apply AM-GM on the terms:\n   \\[\n   y = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{16 \\sin^2 x} + \\frac{12 \\sqrt{3}}{16 \\cos x} \\right) + \\frac{12 \\sqrt{3}}{\\cos x}.\n   \\]\n\n   Notice the term \\( \\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{16\\cos x} \\) contains a factor \\(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4\\cos x}\\), we'll use it to balance the terms:\n   \\[\n   y = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{16 \\sin^2 x} + \\sin^2 x \\right) + \\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{4 \\cos x} + \\cos^2 x - 16.\n   \\]\n\n   Providing an auxiliary check usin