m the origin to this line can be calculated using the formula for the distance from a point to a line in vector form.\n\nGiven a line defined by a point P and direction vector v, the distance from a point Q to the line is |(Q - P) \u00d7 v| / |v|.\n\nIn our case, the line is defined by point A and direction vector (-C). So, the distance from the origin (0) to this line is |(0 - A) \u00d7 (-C)| / | -C | = | (-A) \u00d7 (-C) | / |C| = |A \u00d7 C| / |C|.\n\nSo, the minimal distance is |A \u00d7 C| / |C|.\n\nAccording to the condition, this minimal distance must be \u2265 |C - A|.\n\nTherefore:\n\n|A \u00d7 C| / |C| \u2265 |C - A|\n\nMultiply both sides by |C|:\n\n|A \u00d7 C| \u2265 |C - A| |C|\n\nBut |A \u00d7 C| is the area of the parallelogram spanned by vectors A and C, which is also equal to |A||C| sin \u03b8, where \u03b8 is the angle between A and C (angle at B in triangle ABC). So:\n\n|A||C| sin \u03b8 \u2265 |C - A| |C|\n\nDivide both sides by |C| (assuming |C| \u2260 0, which it is):\n\n|A| sin \u03b8 \u2265 |C - A|\n\nBut |C - A| is the length of side AC in triangle ABC. Let's denote sides:\n\nLet me recall in triangle ABC, with B at the origin, A is vector BA, C is vector BC. Then, side AB is |A|, side BC is |C|, side AC is |C - A|.\n\nSo, in standard triangle notation, let's denote:\n\nAB = c, BC = a, AC = b. Wait, actually, standard notation is usually:\n\nIn triangle ABC,\n\na = BC,\n\nb = AC,\n\nc = AB.\n\nBut maybe to avoid confusion, let's just assign:\n\nAB = |A|,\n\nBC = |C|,\n\nAC = |C - A|.\n\nSo, our inequality is |A| sin \u03b8 \u2265 |C - A|, where \u03b8 is the angle at B.\n\nBut in triangle ABC, by the Law of Sines:\n\nsin \u03b8 / AC = sin(angle at A) / BC = sin(angle at C) / AB\n\nWait, maybe Law of Cosines would help here. Let's recall that in triangle ABC,\n\nAC\u00b2 = AB\u00b2 + BC\u00b2 - 2 AB * BC cos \u03b8.\n\nSo, |C - A|\u00b2 = |A|\u00b2 + |C|\u00b2 - 2 |A||C| cos \u03b8.\n\nBut we have |A| sin \u03b8 \u2265 |C - A|. Let's square