0 => e= -a^3 +a^2 +a.\n\nBut from equation 6: 2a +e=1 => e=1 -2a.\n\nSet equal:\n\n-a^3 +a^2 +a =1 -2a\n\n=> -a^3 +a^2 +3a -1=0.\n\nTrying integer a:\n\na=0: -0 +0 +0 -1= -1\u22600\n\na=1: -1 +1 +3 -1=2\u22600\n\na=2: -8 +4 +6 -1=1\u22600\n\na=3: -27 +9 +9 -1= -10\u22600\n\na=-1: -(-1) +1 + (-3) -1=1 +1 -3 -1= -2\u22600\n\nNo solutions in this case.\n\nCase2: b=2, f=1.\n\nThen equation 7: 2*1=2 (okay).\n\nEquation 6: a*1 +e*2=1 => a +2e=1.\n\nEquation 5: a*e +2*d +1=1 => a*e +2*d=0 => 2d= -a e => d= -a e/2.\n\nBut d must be integer, so -a e must be even.\n\nFrom equation 3:2 +a c +d=1 => a c +d= -1.\n\nFrom equation 2: a +c=1 =>c=1 -a.\n\nFrom equation 4: a d +2 c +e=1.\n\nLet me proceed step by step:\n\nFrom equation 6: a +2e=1 => e=(1 -a)/2.\n\nSince e must be integer, (1 -a) must be even => a is odd.\n\nLet a=2k+1, then e=(1 - (2k+1))/2=(-2k)/2=-k.\n\nSo e= -k.\n\nFrom equation 5: a e +2d=0 => (2k+1)(-k) +2d=0 => -2k^2 -k +2d=0 => 2d=2k^2 +k => d=k^2 +k/2.\n\nSince d must be integer, k must be even. Let k=2m. Then:\n\na=2*(2m)+1=4m+1\n\ne= -k= -2m\n\nd= (2m)^2 + (2m)/2=4m^2 +m.\n\nFrom equation 3: a c +d= -1.\n\nBut c=1 -a=1 -(4m+1)= -4m.\n\nThus, a c +d= (4m+1)(-4m) + (4m^2 +m)= -16m^2 -4m +4m^2 +m= (-12m^2 -3m)= -1.\n\nSo -12m^2 -3m= -1 =>12m^2 +3m=1.\n\nLooking for integer m:\n\n12m^2 +3m -1=0.\n\nDiscriminant=9 +48=57. Not a perfect square. Therefore, no integer solutions.\n\nCase3: b=-1, f=-2.\n\nEquation7: (-1)(-2)=2 (okay).\n\nEquation6: a*(-2) +e*(-1)=1 => -2a -e=1 => e= -2a -1.\n\nEquation5: a*e + (-1)*d + (-2)=1 => a e -d -2=1 => a e -d=3 => d= a e -3.\n\nEquation3: -1 +a c +d=1 => a c +d=2.\n\nFrom equation2: a +c=1 =>c=1 -a.\n\nSubstitute c=1 -a and d= a e -3 into equation3:\n\na*(1 -a) + a e -3=2 => a -a^2 +a e -3=2 => a e= a^2 -a +5.\n\nBut e= -2a -1. Substitute into a e:\n\na*(-2a -1)= -2a^2 -a.\n\nSo -2a^2 -a= a^2 -a +5.\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n-2a^2 -a -a^2 +a -5= -3a^2 -5=0 => 3a^2= -5. No real solutions, let alone integer.\n\nCase4: b=-2