u00b2 + (20738 - 133a)\u00b2 / 1600 = 7396\n\nMultiply both sides by 1600:\n\n1600a\u00b2 + (20738 - 133a)\u00b2 = 7396 * 1600 = 11,833,600\n\nExpand (20738 - 133a)\u00b2:\n\n= 20738\u00b2 - 2*20738*133a + (133a)\u00b2\n\n= 430,004,644 - 5,525,508a + 17,689a\u00b2\n\nThus:\n\n1600a\u00b2 + 430,004,644 - 5,525,508a + 17,689a\u00b2 = 11,833,600\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(1600 + 17,689)a\u00b2 - 5,525,508a + 430,004,644 - 11,833,600 = 0\n\n19,289a\u00b2 - 5,525,508a + 418,171,044 = 0\n\nDivide all terms by GCD (probably 1):\n\n19,289a\u00b2 - 5,525,508a + 418,171,044 = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic equation: ax\u00b2 + bx + c = 0\n\nWhere:\n\na = 19,289\n\nb = -5,525,508\n\nc = 418,171,044\n\nThe discriminant D = b\u00b2 - 4ac\n\nCalculate D:\n\n= (-5,525,508)\u00b2 - 4*19,289*418,171,044\n\nThis is enormous and impractical to compute by hand. Therefore, this approach is not feasible without computational tools.\n\nGiven the time I've invested and the lack of progress, I must conclude that either the problem requires an assumption not stated, or there's a specific property I'm missing. Alternatively, there might be an error in the problem's formulation.\n\nHowever, given that it's a closed polygon and the need to find Reims to Chaumont, the likely intended solution is to recognize that the distance can be found by vector addition of the sides not connecting them. Given that the sum of all vectors is zero, the vector from Reims to Chaumont is equal to the sum of the vectors from Reims to Chalons, Chalons to Vitry, and Vitry to Chaumont. Therefore, the distance would be the magnitude of that sum.\n\nBut without knowing the angles, this magnitude can't be computed. However, if we assume that these vectors form a straight line (which they don't, but perhaps the problem assumes this), the distance would be |40 + 30 + 80| = 150 km. But this is a stretch.\n\nAlternatively, if the path Reims-Chalons-Vitry-Chaumont forms a straight line, then the distance Reims-Chaumont would be 40 