en Distances:**\n    We compute the sum of the distances around the polygon and identify relationships:\n    \\[\n    30 \\, \\text{km} + 80 \\, \\text{km} + 40 \\, \\text{km} + 86 \\, \\text{km} = 236 \\, \\text{km}\n    \\]\n    Clearly, we see that:\n    \\[\n    236 \\, \\text{km} = 30 \\, \\text{km} + 80 \\, \\text{km} + 40 \\, \\text{km} + 86 \\, \\text{km}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Interpret the Relationship:**\n    The equation \\(236 = 30 + 80 + 40 + 86\\) implies that the city distances form a linear sequence on a straight line. Therefore, the cities Ch\u00e2lons, Vitry, Chaumont, Saint-Quentin, and Reims are aligned so that the distance across each segment adds up to form a straight-line distance between origin and endpoint.\n\n4. **Determine Straight-Line Order:**\n    Knowing the cities are on a straight line, we list the sequence from one endpoint to the other:\n    \\[\n    \\text{Saint-Quentin} \\rightarrow \\text{Reims} \\rightarrow \\text{Ch\u00e2lons} \\rightarrow \\text{Vitry} \\rightarrow \\text{Chaumont}\n    \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the Distance from Reims to Chaumont:**\n    To find the distance directly from Reims to Chaumont, add the individual distances between consecutive cities:\n    \\begin{align*}\n    \\text{Reims to Ch\u00e2lons} & : 40 \\, \\text{km} \\\\\n    \\text{Ch\u00e2lons to Vitry} & : 30 \\, \\text{km} \\\\\n    \\text{Vitry to Chaumont} & : 80 \\, \\text{km} \n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore, the distance from Reims to Chaumont is:\n    \\[\n    40 \\, \\text{km} + 30 \\, \\text{km} + 80 \\, \\text{km} = 150 \\, \\text{km}\n    \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{150 \\, \\text{km}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( x \\) be the number of happy subjects, \\( y \\) be the number of redheads, and \\( z \\) be the total number of subjects. According to the given conditions,\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{40}{100} x = \\frac{60}{100} y, \\quad z = x + \\frac{40}{100} y = x + \\frac{2}{5} y \n\\]\n\nWe have \\( 2x = 3y \\). Express all variables through a parameter \\( t \\in \\mathbb{N} \\) :\n\n\\[ \nx = 3t, \\quad y = 2t, \\quad z = 3t + \\frac{4t}{5} = \\frac{19}{5} t \n\\]\n\nSince \\( z \\) is a natural number, \\( t = 5k, k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\), hence \\( x = 15k, y = 10k, z = 19k \\). Then, the number of unhappy redheads is \\( \\frac{2}{5} y = 4k \\). The ratio of the number of unhappy redheads to the total number of subjects is\n\n\\[ \\frac{4k}{19k} = \\frac{4}{19} \\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. So, we have three variables here: x is the number of happy subjects, y is the number of redheads, and z is the total number of subjects. The problem gives us two equations, right?\n\nFirst equation: (40/100)x = (60/100)y. Let me write that down. So, that's 0.4x = 0.6y. Hmm, maybe I can simplify this equation by multiplying both sides by 10 to get rid of the decimals. That would give 4x = 6y. Then, if I divide both sides by 2, it simplifies to 2x = 3y. Okay, so that's a simpler relationship between x and y. That means x is 1.5 times y, right? Because x = (3/2)y. But since we're dealing with people, we can't have fractions of people, so x and y must be integers. That's probably where the parameter t comes in later.\n\nSecond equation: z = x + (40/100)y. Let me check that. So, the total number of subjects z is equal to the number of happy subjects plus 40% of the redheads. Wait, why is that? Maybe the problem states that the total subjects are happy subjects plus some other group, which is 40% of the redheads? Maybe the problem mentions that 40% of redheads are unhappy or something? Hmm, maybe I need to go back to the original problem statement. Wait, the user hasn't provided the actual problem, just the equations and the solution. Hmm. Well, according to the given conditions, z = x + (2/5)y. So, that must be given in the problem. So, z is x plus 40% of y, which is 2/5 of y. Got it.