, the angle at D is angle between DA and BD, and in triangle BCD, the angle at D is angle between CD and BD. Therefore, the sum of these two angles (in triangles ABD and BCD at D) is equal to 130 degrees. Let me denote angle at D in triangle ABD as \u03b1 and in triangle BCD as \u03b2, so \u03b1 + \u03b2 = 130\u00b0.\n\nSimilarly, angle at B in the quadrilateral is 100\u00b0, which is between AB and BC. In triangle ABD, angle at B is between AB and BD, and in triangle BCD, angle at B is between BC and BD. Let me denote those as \u03b3 and \u03b4, so \u03b3 + \u03b4 = 100\u00b0.\n\nIf I can express the angles \u03b3, \u03b4, \u03b1, \u03b2 in terms of sides and apply the Law of Sines or Cosines, maybe I can find x.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using coordinates. Let me try coordinate geometry.\n\nLet me place point B at the origin (0,0). Since AB = 1 and angle at B is 100\u00b0, which is between AB and BC. Let me set AB along the positive x-axis. So point A is at (1,0). Then BC is length 1, making angle 100\u00b0 with AB. Since AB is along the x-axis from B(0,0) to A(1,0), then BC is at an angle of 100\u00b0 from AB. So the direction from B to C is 100\u00b0 from the x-axis. Therefore, point C can be located at coordinates (1*cos(100\u00b0), 1*sin(100\u00b0)). Let me compute that.\n\ncos(100\u00b0) is approximately cos(100\u00b0) \u2248 -0.1736, sin(100\u00b0) \u2248 0.9848. So point C is at approximately (-0.1736, 0.9848). Wait, but AB is from (0,0) to (1,0), so angle at B is 100\u00b0, which is the internal angle. So when moving from AB to BC, the angle is 100\u00b0, meaning that BC is going upwards and to the left from B. So yes, point C would be at (cos(100\u00b0), sin(100\u00b0)) relative to point B. But since angle at B is 100\u00b0, the direction of BC is 100\u00b0 from BA. Wait, BA is along the negative x-axis from B to A. Wait, maybe I need to be careful with the angle measurement.\n\nWait, in standard position, angles are measured from the positive x-axis. If we