38\u0437 \u043d\u0435\u0433\u043e \u043c\u043e\u0436\u043d\u043e \u0432\u044b\u0440\u0435\u0437\u0430\u0442\u044c \u0435\u0449\u0435 \u043e\u0434\u0438\u043d \u0442\u0430\u043a\u043e\u0439 \u043a\u0432\u0430\u0434\u0440\u0430\u0442\u0438\u043a}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "1. Let \\( x, y > 0 \\) and \\( p, q \\in [1,+\\infty[ \\) such that \\( \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1 \\). Show that:\n\n\\[ \nxy \\leq \\frac{x^p}{p} + \\frac{y^q}{q}\n\\]\n\n2. Let \\( x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\) and \\( y_1, \\ldots, y_n \\) be strictly positive real numbers. Given \\( p, q \\in [1,+\\infty[ \\) such that \\( \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1 \\). Show that:\n\n\\[ \n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i y_i \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}} \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} y_i^q \\right)^{\\frac{1}{q}}\n\\]\n\n3. Show that:\n\n\\[ \n\\left(1 + x^2 y + x^4 y^2 \\right)^3 \\leq \\left(1 + x^3 + x^6 \\right)^2 \\left(1 + y^3 + y^6 \\right)\n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's tackle these three problems one by one. They all seem related to inequalities, which makes me think of H\u00f6lder's inequality, Young's inequality, and maybe AM-GM. Let's start with the first one.\n\n**Problem 1: Show that \\( xy \\leq \\frac{x^p}{p} + \\frac{y^q}{q} \\) where \\( x, y > 0 \\) and \\( \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1 \\).**\n\nOkay, this looks familiar. I remember that Young's inequality states exactly this. The standard proof uses convexity or the AM-GM inequality. Let me recall. For conjugate exponents p and q (which satisfy 1/p + 1/q = 1), the inequality is a form of Young's inequality. \n\nOne way to prove it is by using the weighted AM-GM inequality. The idea is that for positive numbers a and b, and weights that sum to 1, the weighted AM is greater than or equal to the weighted GM. Here, if we set a = x^p, b = y^q, and the weights as 1/p and 1/q, then:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{p} \\cdot x^p + \\frac{1}{q} \\cdot y^q \\geq x^{p \\cdot (1/p)} y^{q \\cdot (1/q)} = x y\n\\]\n\nWait, let me check that again. The exponents in the GM part should be the weights. So weighted AM-GM is:\n\n\\[\n\\lambda a + (1 - \\lambda) b \\geq a^{\\lambda} b^{1 - \\lambda}\n\\]\n\nfor \\( \\lambda \\in (0,1) \\). In our case, \\( \\lambda = 1/p \\), so \\( 1 - \\lambda = 1/q \\), since \\( 1/p + 1/q = 1 \\). Then substituting a = x^p and b = y^q,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{p} x^p + \\frac{1}{q} y^q \\geq (x^p)^{1/p} (y^q)^{1/q} = x y\n\\]\n\nYes, that's exactly the inequality we need. So that's the proof. So Problem 1 is an application of the weighted AM-GM inequality, which is essentially Young's inequality.\n\n**Problem 2: H\u00f6lder's inequality for sequences.**\n\nWe need to show that:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i y_i \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^p \\right)^{1/p} \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} y_i^q \\right)^{1/q}\n\\]\n\nGiven that p and q are conjugate exponents (1/p + 1/q = 1). \n\nH\u00f6lder's inequality! Yes, this is the standard form of H\u00f6lder's inequality for sequences. The proof often uses Young's inequality from Problem 1. Let me recall the approach.\n\nThe idea is to normalize the sequences by dividing each term by the norm, so that we can apply Young's inequality term-wise. Let me write down the steps.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n\\[\nA = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^p \\right)^{1/p}, \\quad B = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i^q \\right)^{1/q}\n\\]\n\nWe need to show that:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i y_i \\leq A B\n\\]\n\nIf either A or B is zero, then all terms in the respective sums would be zero (since x_i, y_i are positive), so the inequality holds. So assume A, B > 0.\n\nDefine normalized variables:\n\n\\[\nu_i = \\frac{x_i}{A}, \\quad v_i = \\frac{y_i}{B}\n\\]\n\nThen, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n u_i^p = \\frac{1}{A^p} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^p = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n v_i^q = \\frac{1}{B^q} \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i^q = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, apply Young's inequality (from Problem 1) to each term u_i v_i:\n\n\\[